import"./NZTpNUN0.js";import"./BkvxQF8z.js";import{t,q as o,P as l}from"./D3cAVrQp.js";var n=t(`<h1>Chapter 16: Building the Squad</h1> <p><strong>Turning Discord servers, gaming communities, and existing networks into political forces</strong></p> <p>You’ve learned how to infiltrate institutions (Chapter 15).</p> <p>Now you need the other half of power: <strong>organized people.</strong></p> <p>Not “someday when we have thousands of members.”
Not “after we build the perfect organizational structure.”</p> <p><strong>Monday morning. With the 20-50 people you already know.</strong></p> <p>Because here’s what the oligarchy doesn’t want you to realize:</p> <p><strong>You’re already organized.</strong> You just haven’t weaponized your existing networks for political power.</p> <hr> <h2>The Hidden Army: Networks You Already Have</h2> <p>Right now, you probably have access to:</p> <p><strong>Digital networks:</strong></p> <ul><li>Discord servers (gaming, hobbies, learning)</li> <li>WhatsApp/Signal groups (friends, family, work)</li> <li>Reddit communities (niche interests)</li> <li>Twitter/X mutuals (shared political concerns)</li> <li>Facebook groups (neighborhood, school, alumni)</li></ul> <p><strong>Physical networks:</strong></p> <ul><li>Gym crews (people you see 3x/week)</li> <li>Parents at school gates (mutual childcare needs)</li> <li>Pub regulars (people you actually know, not just drink near)</li> <li>Running/cycling clubs (shared endurance, trust)</li> <li>Book clubs, gaming groups, hobby meetups</li></ul> <p><strong>Professional networks:</strong></p> <ul><li>Former colleagues (shared work experience)</li> <li>Professional associations (LinkedIn connections that matter)</li> <li>Union members (already organized labor)</li> <li>Industry groups (conferences, Slack channels)</li></ul> <p><strong>The insight:</strong> These networks already have:</p> <ul><li><strong>Trust</strong> (you know these people)</li> <li><strong>Coordination</strong> (you already meet regularly)</li> <li><strong>Communication</strong> (established channels)</li> <li><strong>Shared interests</strong> (reasons you’re connected)</li></ul> <p><strong>What they lack:</strong> Political direction. Organized power. Strategic focus.</p> <p><strong>What this chapter teaches:</strong> How to add those three elements without destroying what makes the network valuable in the first place.</p> <hr> <h2>The Core Strategy: Activity → Identity → Politics</h2> <p><strong>The wrong approach:</strong> Show up in your gaming Discord and announce “We’re organizing for revolution now!”</p> <p><strong>Result:</strong> Everyone thinks you’re weird, leaves the server, or worst case—you’re banned.</p> <p><strong>The right approach:</strong> Gradual escalation through three stages.</p> <h3>Stage 1: Activity (What Brought You Together)</h3> <p><strong>Months 1-3: Just do the thing you already do, but slightly more intentionally.</strong></p> <p><strong>For gaming Discord:</strong></p> <ul><li>Keep playing games (obviously)</li> <li>But maybe organize tournaments with small buy-ins</li> <li>Or start “meta” discussions about game mechanics, balancing, systems</li> <li>Or create roles/channels for different game types</li></ul> <p><strong>For gym crew:</strong></p> <ul><li>Keep working out together</li> <li>But maybe coordinate meal prep Sundays</li> <li>Or share workout programs, technique videos</li> <li>Or organize group challenges (30-day streak, PR competitions)</li></ul> <p><strong>For parents group:</strong></p> <ul><li>Keep doing childcare coordination</li> <li>But maybe create WhatsApp schedule for pickups/dropoffs</li> <li>Or organize group outings to park, museum</li> <li>Or share resources (hand-me-downs, recommendations)</li></ul> <p><strong>The principle:</strong> Increase the <strong>density and quality</strong> of existing activity. Make the network more valuable for its stated purpose.</p> <p><strong>Why this works:</strong> You’re not threatening the network’s reason for existing. You’re enhancing it. Trust and coordination deepen naturally.</p> <h3>Stage 2: Identity (Naming Who We Are)</h3> <p><strong>Months 3-6: Start giving the group a sense of itself as a thing.</strong></p> <p><strong>For gaming Discord:</strong></p> <ul><li>Create server logo/icon</li> <li>Develop inside jokes, memes, traditions</li> <li>Host events (game nights with themes)</li> <li>Recognize members (award titles, badges for participation)</li> <li>Maybe get domain name, simple website</li></ul> <p><strong>For gym crew:</strong></p> <ul><li>Name yourselves (“The 6AM Crew,” “Iron Temple Regulars”)</li> <li>Get matching shirts (only half joking)</li> <li>Take progress photos together</li> <li>Create shared goals (“Everyone hits bodyweight lift by December”)</li></ul> <p><strong>For parents group:</strong></p> <ul><li>Become “official” (group chat → Signal group with name)</li> <li>Regular events (monthly potluck, quarterly outing)</li> <li>Shared resources (Google Doc of recommendations, equipment share)</li> <li>Recognition (celebrate birthdays, acknowledge contributions)</li></ul> <p><strong>The principle:</strong> Transform “people who do X” into “we are the X people.” From activity to identity.</p> <p><strong>Why this works:</strong> Identity creates loyalty beyond utility. People stay because they’re part of something, not just because it’s convenient.</p> <p><strong>Warning:</strong> Don’t force this. Let it emerge organically from the activity. You’re just naming and formalizing what’s already happening.</p> <h3>Stage 3: Politics (What We Care About Beyond Ourselves)</h3> <p><strong>Months 6-12: Gradually introduce shared concerns, then collective action.</strong></p> <p><strong>The gentle escalation:</strong></p> <p><strong>Phase A: Shared Problems (Months 6-8)</strong></p> <p>Start noticing collective challenges that affect your group:</p> <p><strong>For gaming Discord:</strong></p> <ul><li>“This new loot box system is predatory gambling”</li> <li>“These patch notes show they don’t actually play their own game”</li> <li>“Servers are down again—why are we paying subscriptions for this?”</li></ul> <p><strong>For gym crew:</strong></p> <ul><li>“Rent’s going up 30%, might have to move gyms”</li> <li>“New manager is killing the vibe, considering alternatives”</li> <li>“Equipment broken again, nobody maintains anything”</li></ul> <p><strong>For parents group:</strong></p> <ul><li>“School cutting arts program due to budget”</li> <li>“Housing costs mean kids sharing bedrooms well into teens”</li> <li>“No safe playground within walking distance”</li></ul> <p><strong>The principle:</strong> Notice shared frustrations. Frame them as collective problems, not individual complaints.</p> <p><strong>Phase B: Collective Analysis (Months 8-10)</strong></p> <p>Start analyzing the systems causing these problems:</p> <p><strong>For gaming Discord:</strong></p> <ul><li>“It’s not just this game—all games doing this now”</li> <li>“Publishers optimizing for addiction, not fun”</li> <li>“We’re the product being sold to investors”</li> <li>Share articles, videos analyzing attention economy</li></ul> <p><strong>For gym crew:</strong></p> <ul><li>“It’s not just our gym—commercial fitness extractive everywhere”</li> <li>“Landlords raising rent because they can, not because costs increased”</li> <li>“Working more hours, less time for health—something’s wrong with that”</li></ul> <p><strong>For parents group:</strong></p> <ul><li>“It’s not just our school—austerity gutting all public services”</li> <li>“Housing crisis is policy choice, not natural market force”</li> <li>“We’re doing childcare coordination because social safety net disappeared”</li></ul> <p><strong>The principle:</strong> Move from “our specific problem” to “systemic patterns.” Develop shared analysis without being preachy.</p> <p><strong>Phase C: Coordinated Action (Months 10-12)</strong></p> <p>Start small collective actions:</p> <p><strong>For gaming Discord:</strong></p> <ul><li>Boycott new expansion together</li> <li>Write collective feedback/petition to developers</li> <li>Support indie games that respect players</li> <li>Maybe: Organize local gaming meetup, LAN party</li></ul> <p><strong>For gym crew:</strong></p> <ul><li>Negotiate group discount with management</li> <li>Research cooperative gym models</li> <li>Support each other job searching (so less trapped by location)</li> <li>Maybe: Visit cooperative gym in nearby city</li></ul> <p><strong>For parents group:</strong></p> <ul><li>Attend school board meeting together</li> <li>Submit collective budget proposal</li> <li>Organize community childcare rotation (formalize mutual aid)</li> <li>Maybe: Research community land trust for affordable housing</li></ul> <p><strong>The principle:</strong> Take action together. Start small (petitions, meetings) before escalating to bigger commitments.</p> <p><strong>The result:</strong> By Month 12, you have a group that:</p> <ul><li>Still does the original activity (games, gym, parenting)</li> <li>Has strong identity and loyalty</li> <li>Understands systemic problems</li> <li>Takes coordinated political action</li> <li><strong>And doesn’t feel like it changed—it feels like it evolved naturally</strong></li></ul> <hr> <h2>The Squad Health Dashboard: Tracking Your Progress</h2> <p><strong>Before diving into specific network types, here’s your accountability tool.</strong></p> <p>Use this to avoid the “talking without action” trap and maintain momentum:</p> <table><thead><tr><th>Metric</th><th>Week 4</th><th>Week 8</th><th>Week 12</th><th>Month 6</th><th>Month 12</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Ring 1 members</strong></td><td>3</td><td>5</td><td>7</td><td>8-12</td><td>10-15</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Ring 2 active</strong></td><td>5</td><td>15</td><td>25</td><td>30-40</td><td>40-60</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Ring 3 engaged</strong></td><td>20</td><td>60</td><td>150</td><td>200-300</td><td>400-600</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Events held</strong></td><td>1</td><td>3</td><td>6</td><td>12</td><td>24</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Actions completed</strong></td><td>1</td><td>2</td><td>4</td><td>8</td><td>15+</td></tr><tr><td><strong>External wins</strong></td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>2</td><td>4-6</td><td>10+</td></tr></tbody></table> <p><strong>What counts as an “external win”:</strong></p> <ul><li>Council/institution changed policy you advocated</li> <li>Media coverage of your position</li> <li>Successful campaign (boycott, petition delivered)</li> <li>New institution created (cooperative, land trust)</li> <li>Electoral victory (candidate you supported won)</li> <li>Resource secured (grant, space, partnership)</li></ul> <p><strong>When to pivot:</strong></p> <p>🚨 <strong>If Ring 1 has met 3+ months with no external action → PIVOT TO ACTION</strong></p> <ul><li>You’re in talking trap</li> <li>Pick smallest viable action and do it this week</li> <li>Morale collapses without wins</li></ul> <p>🚨 <strong>If Ring 2 is stagnant (same 5 people doing everything) → REINVIGORATE</strong></p> <ul><li>Host new events targeting different audiences</li> <li>Create new roles/working groups</li> <li>Invite specific people to specific tasks</li> <li>Ring 2 stagnation means you’re not recruiting well</li></ul> <p>🚨 <strong>If Ring 3 isn’t growing → ADJUST CONTENT/EVENTS</strong></p> <ul><li>Your messaging isn’t resonating</li> <li>Your events aren’t accessible/interesting</li> <li>Try different formats, topics, venues</li> <li>Ask current Ring 3: “What would you invite friends to?”</li></ul> <p><strong>Quarterly audit:</strong> Every 3 months, fill in your dashboard and check trajectory. If numbers declining or stagnant, diagnose and fix.</p> <hr> <h2>The Five Network Types and How to Activate Each</h2> <p>Different networks have different activation pathways. Here’s the playbook for each:</p> <h3>Network Type 1: Gaming/Online Communities</h3> <p><strong>Current state:</strong></p> <ul><li>Coordinated for entertainment</li> <li>High trust within game context</li> <li>Communication infrastructure (Discord, forums)</li> <li>Often young, digitally native, understands systems thinking</li></ul> <p><strong>Activation pathway:</strong></p> <p><strong>Month 1-3: Enhance gaming experience</strong></p> <ul><li>Better tournament organization</li> <li>Strategy guides, meta analysis</li> <li>Community events, inside jokes</li> <li>Increase activity density</li></ul> <p><strong>Month 3-6: Develop identity</strong></p> <ul><li>Server branding, traditions</li> <li>Recognition systems (roles, titles)</li> <li>Regular events beyond just playing</li> <li>“We are [Server Name]” consciousness</li></ul> <p><strong>Month 6-9: Notice exploitation</strong></p> <ul><li>Loot boxes = gambling</li> <li>Battle passes = engineered addiction</li> <li>Data harvesting, attention extraction</li> <li>Frame as systems analysis (gamers understand this)</li></ul> <p><strong>Month 9-12: Collective responses</strong></p> <ul><li>Boycotts of predatory games</li> <li>Support indie/ethical developers</li> <li>Digital organizing skills → civic organizing</li> <li>Maybe: Local meetups, IRL coordination</li></ul> <p><strong>The bridge to politics:</strong></p> <ul><li>Gamers understand systems (game mechanics)</li> <li>Gamers understand exploitation (pay-to-win)</li> <li>Gamers understand coordination (raids, guilds)</li> <li><strong>You’re just applying game thinking to political terrain</strong></li></ul> <p><strong>Real example:</strong> Reddit communities organizing GameStop short squeeze (2021)</p> <ul><li>Existed for market discussion (activity)</li> <li>Developed identity (“apes,” “diamond hands”)</li> <li>Noticed hedge fund exploitation</li> <li>Coordinated massive collective action</li> <li>Successfully extracted billions from finance</li></ul> <p><strong>Your version:</strong> Take Discord server from gaming → systems analysis → civic action</p> <h3>Network Type 2: Physical Fitness Groups</h3> <p><strong>Current state:</strong></p> <ul><li>Meet 3-7x/week (very high trust building)</li> <li>Shared suffering creates bonding</li> <li>Goal-oriented, results-focused</li> <li>Often diverse across age, class, politics</li></ul> <p><strong>Activation pathway:</strong></p> <p><strong>Month 1-3: Deepen fitness coordination</strong></p> <ul><li>Group challenges, competitions</li> <li>Meal prep coordination</li> <li>Technique sharing, form checks</li> <li>Partner up for accountability</li></ul> <p><strong>Month 3-6: Build crew identity</strong></p> <ul><li>Name yourselves</li> <li>Shared goals (everyone hits milestone)</li> <li>Social beyond gym (post-workout meals)</li> <li>Recognize progress, celebrate wins</li></ul> <p><strong>Month 6-9: Notice economic pressures</strong></p> <ul><li>Rent increases forcing location changes</li> <li>Working more, less time for health</li> <li>Gym extracting maximum while quality declines</li> <li>Healthcare costs when injuries happen</li></ul> <p><strong>Month 9-12: Collective solutions</strong></p> <ul><li>Negotiate with management together</li> <li>Research cooperative fitness models</li> <li>Support each other’s economic struggles</li> <li>Maybe: Form actual cooperative gym</li></ul> <p><strong>The bridge to politics:</strong></p> <ul><li>Fitness people understand discipline</li> <li>Fitness people understand long-term results</li> <li>Fitness people understand teamwork</li> <li><strong>You’re just applying training mentality to political terrain</strong></li></ul> <p><strong>Real example:</strong> CrossFit gyms that became community hubs (pre-2020)</p> <ul><li>Started as workout groups</li> <li>Became tight social networks</li> <li>Some evolved into mutual aid during COVID</li> <li>Now some are worker-owned cooperatives</li></ul> <p><strong>Your version:</strong> Take gym crew from workouts → economic analysis → cooperative ownership</p> <h3>Network Type 3: Parents/Caregivers Groups</h3> <p><strong>Current state:</strong></p> <ul><li>Mutual aid already happening (childcare swaps)</li> <li>High stakes (children’s wellbeing)</li> <li>Diverse politics but shared concerns</li> <li>Regular coordination (school, activities)</li></ul> <p><strong>Activation pathway:</strong></p> <p><strong>Month 1-3: Formalize mutual aid</strong></p> <ul><li>WhatsApp group → organized schedule</li> <li>Pickup/dropoff coordination</li> <li>Equipment/clothing share system</li> <li>Group outings (split transport, costs)</li></ul> <p><strong>Month 3-6: Build community identity</strong></p> <ul><li>Regular gatherings (monthly potluck)</li> <li>Shared resources (recommendations doc)</li> <li>Support system (celebrate birthdays, hard times)</li> <li>“Our kids grow up together” consciousness</li></ul> <p><strong>Month 6-9: Notice systemic failures</strong></p> <ul><li>School budget cuts affecting kids</li> <li>Housing costs shrinking family space</li> <li>Both parents working, less time with children</li> <li>Isolated nuclear families doing all care alone</li></ul> <p><strong>Month 9-12: Collective advocacy</strong></p> <ul><li>Attend school board meetings together</li> <li>Organize community childcare rotation</li> <li>Research cooperative housing models</li> <li>Maybe: Form actual childcare cooperative</li></ul> <p><strong>The bridge to politics:</strong></p> <ul><li>Parents fiercely protect children’s interests</li> <li>Parents understand long-term consequences</li> <li>Parents already doing mutual aid</li> <li><strong>You’re just formalizing and scaling what’s already happening</strong></li></ul> <p><strong>Real example:</strong> Parent-led school transformations (UK)</p> <ul><li>Started as PTA volunteers</li> <li>Noticed budget inadequacies</li> <li>Organized to pressure council/MPs</li> <li>Some evolved into parent-run cooperatives</li></ul> <p><strong>Your version:</strong> Take parents group from childcare swaps → political advocacy → cooperative institutions</p> <h3>Network Type 4: Professional/Industry Groups</h3> <p><strong>Current state:</strong></p> <ul><li>Shared expertise, industry knowledge</li> <li>Loose ties (conferences, LinkedIn)</li> <li>Career-focused, often competitive</li> <li>Understanding of how their sector actually works</li></ul> <p><strong>Activation pathway:</strong></p> <p><strong>Month 1-3: Increase professional value</strong></p> <ul><li>Start Slack channel or regular calls</li> <li>Share opportunities, resources</li> <li>Skill-sharing, problem-solving</li> <li>Mentorship between experienced/junior</li></ul> <p><strong>Month 3-6: Develop professional identity</strong></p> <ul><li>“We are [Industry] professionals who care about quality”</li> <li>Regular meetups (happy hours, conferences)</li> <li>Collective reputation building</li> <li>Support each other’s projects</li></ul> <p><strong>Month 6-9: Notice industry problems</strong></p> <ul><li>Race to bottom on quality</li> <li>Clients demanding more for less</li> <li>Exploitation of junior staff</li> <li>Industry consolidation reducing opportunity</li></ul> <p><strong>Month 9-12: Collective professional power</strong></p> <ul><li>Form professional cooperative</li> <li>Set ethical standards together</li> <li>Collective bargaining with clients</li> <li>Maybe: Launch cooperative consultancy/firm</li></ul> <p><strong>The bridge to politics:</strong></p> <ul><li>Professionals understand systems (they work in them)</li> <li>Professionals understand extraction (they experience it)</li> <li>Professionals have expertise (valuable contribution)</li> <li><strong>You’re just applying professional standards to political economy</strong></li></ul> <p><strong>Real example:</strong> Tech workers organizing (2016-present)</p> <ul><li>Started in industry Slack channels</li> <li>Noticed unethical projects (ICE contracts, military)</li> <li>Coordinated internal opposition</li> <li>Some formed ethical tech cooperatives</li></ul> <p><strong>Your version:</strong> Take professional network from career support → ethical standards → cooperative firms</p> <h3>Network Type 5: Neighborhood/Local Groups</h3> <p><strong>Current state:</strong></p> <ul><li>Geographic proximity</li> <li>Shared local concerns (traffic, safety, development)</li> <li>Often fragmented (NextDoor drama)</li> <li>Mix of politics, ages, backgrounds</li></ul> <p><strong>Activation pathway:</strong></p> <p><strong>Month 1-3: Build local connections</strong></p> <ul><li>Organize street cleanup, garden day</li> <li>Block parties, social events</li> <li>WhatsApp group for local updates</li> <li>Support local businesses together</li></ul> <p><strong>Month 3-6: Develop neighborhood identity</strong></p> <ul><li>“We are [Neighborhood Name] residents”</li> <li>Regular events (seasonal gatherings)</li> <li>Shared resources (tool library, book swap)</li> <li>Look out for each other (elder care, youth mentoring)</li></ul> <p><strong>Month 6-9: Notice local extraction</strong></p> <ul><li>Landlords raising rents, evicting long-term residents</li> <li>Developers building luxury, displacing community</li> <li>Local shops replaced by chains</li> <li>Council neglecting area, prioritizing elsewhere</li></ul> <p><strong>Month 9-12: Collective territorial power</strong></p> <ul><li>Attend council planning meetings together</li> <li>Support local businesses, boycott extractive ones</li> <li>Research community land trusts</li> <li>Maybe: Form neighborhood association with real power</li></ul> <p><strong>The bridge to politics:</strong></p> <ul><li>Neighbors share immediate concerns</li> <li>Neighbors see extraction directly</li> <li>Neighbors already coordinate informally</li> <li><strong>You’re just formalizing territorial power</strong></li></ul> <p><strong>Real example:</strong> London Community Land Trust (2008-present)</p> <ul><li>Started as residents noticing displacement</li> <li>Organized to pressure council</li> <li>Formed CLT to preserve affordable housing</li> <li>Now own multiple properties, model replicating</li></ul> <p><strong>Your version:</strong> Take neighborhood from social group → organized residents → community ownership</p> <hr> <h2>Tactical Conversion Protocols: Network-Specific Moves</h2> <p><strong>The insight:</strong> Each network type requires different tactical approach. Here are the specific moves that work:</p> <h3>Discord/Gaming Communities: From Chatting to Doing</h3> <p><strong>The problem:</strong> Gaming Discords are high-activity, high-coordination—but for games, not politics. How do you redirect that energy?</p> <p><strong>The conversion protocol:</strong></p> <p><strong>Step 1: Create locked channel (Month 3-4)</strong></p> <ul><li>Name it something appropriate: <code>#meta-analysis</code> or <code>#real-world-guild</code> or <code>#war-room</code></li> <li>Invite only 5 most competent/trusted members</li> <li>Frame: “Want to work on something real, not just games?”</li></ul> <p><strong>Step 2: Give specific project (Month 4-6)</strong></p> <ul><li>Not “let’s organize”—too vague</li> <li>Specific: “Map local council property ownership” or “Research community broadband” or “Analyze school funding”</li> <li>Use their existing skills (research, data analysis, system understanding)</li> <li>Deliverable in 4-6 weeks</li></ul> <p><strong>Step 3: Present findings publicly (Month 6-8)</strong></p> <ul><li>Create document, video, infographic</li> <li>Share in main server (show what #war-room produces)</li> <li>“This is what we found. Here’s what it means.”</li> <li>Some main server people will ask to join</li></ul> <p><strong>Step 4: Execute action (Month 8-10)</strong></p> <ul><li>Based on research, take real-world action</li> <li>Could be: Attend council meeting, launch petition, contact journalist</li> <li>Document and share</li> <li>Now you’re doing politics, not just analyzing</li></ul> <p><strong>The key:</strong> Gaming communities already understand:</p> <ul><li><strong>Systems</strong> (game mechanics = political economy)</li> <li><strong>Coordination</strong> (raids = protests)</li> <li><strong>Optimization</strong> (min-maxing = strategic organizing)</li></ul> <p>You’re just redirecting existing competencies toward political terrain.</p> <p><strong>Real tactic example:</strong></p> <ul><li>Discord researches which games have most predatory monetization</li> <li>Creates comprehensive analysis with data</li> <li>Coordinates mass refund requests</li> <li>Gets gaming media coverage</li> <li>Studio changes policy</li> <li>→ Now they know: Coordination works. What else can we coordinate?</li></ul> <h3>Gym/Sports Crews: From Activity to Identity to Territory</h3> <p><strong>The problem:</strong> Gym people are disciplined, goal-oriented—but focused inward (personal gains). How do you pivot to collective action?</p> <p><strong>The conversion protocol:</strong></p> <p><strong>Step 1: Create identity artifact (Month 3-4)</strong></p> <ul><li>Name yourselves (don’t just be “people who go to gym”)</li> <li>Example: “The 6AM Crew” or “Iron Temple Squad”</li> <li>Maybe get matching shirts (sounds corny, but identity markers work)</li> <li>Frame: “We’re not just individuals working out near each other—we’re a crew”</li></ul> <p><strong>Step 2: Collective challenge with external element (Month 4-6)</strong></p> <ul><li>Not just “everyone hit PR”—too individual</li> <li>Collective: “Crew average of X lifts by [date]”</li> <li>External element: “Clean up park where we run” or “Organize free fitness class for community”</li> <li>This shifts from “I improve me” to “we do something together”</li></ul> <p><strong>Step 3: Notice territorial threat (Month 6-8)</strong></p> <ul><li>Rent increasing, might lose gym access</li> <li>Park where you run getting privatized</li> <li>Council cutting public fitness resources</li> <li>Frame: “Our space is being taken. What do we do?”</li></ul> <p><strong>Step 4: Collective defense (Month 8-10)</strong></p> <ul><li>Negotiate with gym management together (collective bargaining)</li> <li>Attend council meeting about park privatization</li> <li>Research cooperative gym models</li> <li>Now you’re defending shared territory</li></ul> <p><strong>The key:</strong> Fitness people already understand:</p> <ul><li><strong>Discipline</strong> (showing up consistently)</li> <li><strong>Long-term thinking</strong> (gains take months/years)</li> <li><strong>Collective motivation</strong> (training partners push each other)</li></ul> <p>You’re just applying training mentality to political organizing.</p> <p><strong>Real tactic example:</strong></p> <ul><li>Gym crew’s rent raised 30%</li> <li>Group researches: “What would cooperative ownership cost?”</li> <li>Discovers: £50K buyout, spread across 100 members = £500 each</li> <li>Organizes fundraising: “Own our gym instead of renting”</li> <li>Successfully transitions to cooperative</li> <li>→ Now they know: Collective ownership possible. What else can we own?</li></ul> <h3>Parents Groups: From Complaining to Governing</h3> <p><strong>The problem:</strong> Parents groups have high stakes (children’s wellbeing) and mutual aid already happening—but often just venting, not organizing. How do you move to power?</p> <p><strong>The conversion protocol:</strong></p> <p><strong>Step 1: Formalize mutual aid (Month 3-4)</strong></p> <ul><li>WhatsApp group → Named Signal group with schedule</li> <li>“Childcare Collective” or ”[Neighborhood] Parents Network”</li> <li>Create actual rotation system (Google Calendar)</li> <li>Document who helps whom (builds reciprocity, accountability)</li></ul> <p><strong>Step 2: Identify shared threat (Month 4-6)</strong></p> <ul><li>School cutting program parents care about</li> <li>Playground safety issue council ignoring</li> <li>Housing costs forcing families to move away</li> <li>Frame: “This affects all our kids. What can we do together?”</li></ul> <p><strong>Step 3: Collective research (Month 6-8)</strong></p> <ul><li>Understand the decision-making process (who decides? when?)</li> <li>Research budgets, alternatives, precedents from other areas</li> <li>Create proposal with specific asks</li> <li>“We’re not just complaining—we have solution”</li></ul> <p><strong>Step 4: Take institutional action (Month 8-10)</strong></p> <ul><li>Attend school board meeting together (10+ parents showing up is pressure)</li> <li>Meet with councillors (representing organized constituent group)</li> <li>Run for school board (some parents need to be inside)</li> <li>Now you’re governing, not just requesting</li></ul> <p><strong>The key:</strong> Parents already have:</p> <ul><li><strong>High stakes motivation</strong> (will do anything for kids)</li> <li><strong>Mutual aid practice</strong> (childcare swaps, resource sharing)</li> <li><strong>Legitimacy</strong> (representing children, not just self-interest)</li></ul> <p>You’re just directing existing energy toward formal power.</p> <p><strong>Real tactic example:</strong></p> <ul><li>School announces arts program cut due to “budget constraints”</li> <li>Parents group researches: “How is budget actually allocated?”</li> <li>Discovers: £200K going to consultants, arts program costs £40K</li> <li>Creates counter-proposal: “Cut consultants, keep arts”</li> <li>Presents at board meeting with 30 parents attending</li> <li>Board reverses decision under pressure</li> <li>→ Now they know: Organized parents have power. What else can we change?</li></ul> <h3>Professional Networks: From Networking to Cooperating</h3> <p><strong>The problem:</strong> Professional networks are transactional (career advancement, opportunities)—how do you build actual solidarity?</p> <p><strong>The conversion protocol:</strong></p> <p><strong>Step 1: Increase professional value (Month 3-4)</strong></p> <ul><li>Start Slack channel or monthly calls</li> <li>Share opportunities, skills, client leads</li> <li>Frame: “We’re stronger together than competing”</li> <li>Build reciprocity (I help you, you help me)</li></ul> <p><strong>Step 2: Notice shared exploitation (Month 4-6)</strong></p> <ul><li>Clients demanding more work for same pay</li> <li>Industry race to bottom on rates</li> <li>Corporations undercutting independent professionals</li> <li>Frame: “Alone we can’t resist this. Together?”</li></ul> <p><strong>Step 3: Create collective standards (Month 6-8)</strong></p> <ul><li>Agree on minimum rates (no undercutting each other)</li> <li>Share client red flags (warn others about bad actors)</li> <li>Develop quality standards (differentiate on quality, not price)</li> <li>“We’re professional collective setting ethical baseline”</li></ul> <p><strong>Step 4: Form cooperative structure (Month 8-12)</strong></p> <ul><li>Legal entity (cooperative, CIC, partnership)</li> <li>Collective client negotiations (“hire us as team”)</li> <li>Shared resources (office, equipment, admin)</li> <li>Worker ownership (democratic, not hierarchical)</li></ul> <p><strong>The key:</strong> Professionals already understand:</p> <ul><li><strong>Value of expertise</strong> (know what their work is worth)</li> <li><strong>Industry dynamics</strong> (see systemic problems clearly)</li> <li><strong>Coordination benefits</strong> (collaboration > competition)</li></ul> <p>You’re just formalizing collective bargaining power.</p> <p><strong>Real tactic example:</strong></p> <ul><li>Freelance designers noticing clients demanding more revisions, lower rates</li> <li>Form “Design Cooperative” with 8 members</li> <li>Set minimum rate: £500/day, max 3 revision rounds</li> <li>Clients can’t play them against each other anymore</li> <li>Some clients leave, but remaining ones respect boundaries</li> <li>Cooperative more profitable per member than solo work</li> <li>→ Now they know: Collective bargaining works. What about other industries?</li></ul> <hr> <h2>The Drama Firewall: Conflict Without Implosion</h2> <p><strong>The question:</strong> How do you scale from small trusted group to political force?</p> <p><strong>The wrong answer:</strong> Recruit constantly, build mass organization, become NGO.</p> <p><strong>The right answer:</strong> Cell structure with clear levels of commitment.</p> <h3>The Three Rings: Organizing by Commitment Level</h3> <p>Think of your organization like concentric circles:</p> <p><strong>Ring 1: The Core Squad (5-15 people)</strong></p> <ul><li>High trust, high commitment</li> <li>Meet weekly (in-person or video)</li> <li>Handle strategy, money, sensitive decisions</li> <li>These are your co-conspirators</li></ul> <p><strong>Ring 2: Active Participants (20-50 people)</strong></p> <ul><li>Medium trust, regular engagement</li> <li>Participate in actions, events, projects</li> <li>Don’t make strategic decisions but execute reliably</li> <li>These are your activists/members</li></ul> <p><strong>Ring 3: Supportive Community (50-500+ people)</strong></p> <ul><li>Low commitment, episodic engagement</li> <li>Show up for big events, share content</li> <li>Provide legitimacy, numbers, resources</li> <li>These are your movement/network</li></ul> <p><strong>The principle:</strong> Most energy goes to Ring 1. Ring 2 gets assignments. Ring 3 gets invitations.</p> <p><strong>Why this works:</strong></p> <ul><li>Protects against infiltration (only Ring 1 knows strategy)</li> <li>Efficient use of organizer time (focus where highest trust)</li> <li>Allows scaling without losing coherence (clear structure)</li> <li>Maintains operational security (compartmentalization)</li></ul> <h3>Ring 1: The Core Squad — How to Build</h3> <p><strong>Who should be in Ring 1:</strong></p> <p><strong>Criteria:</strong></p> <ul class="contains-task-list"><li class="task-list-item"><input type="checkbox" disabled> You’d trust them with your money</li> <li class="task-list-item"><input type="checkbox" disabled> You’ve seen them under stress (they didn’t flake)</li> <li class="task-list-item"><input type="checkbox" disabled> They have useful skills (organizing, tech, writing, law, etc.)</li> <li class="task-list-item"><input type="checkbox" disabled> They’re committed to long game (not just momentary outrage)</li> <li class="task-list-item"><input type="checkbox" disabled> They can disagree productively (no ego drama)</li></ul> <p><strong>Red flags (do NOT include in Ring 1):</strong></p> <ul><li>Chaotic energy (exciting but unreliable)</li> <li>Savior complex (wants to be hero, not team player)</li> <li>Can’t keep confidence (shares sensitive info casually)</li> <li>Conflict-averse (won’t give honest feedback)</li> <li>Needs constant validation (emotional labor sink)</li></ul> <p><strong>How to form Ring 1:</strong></p> <p><strong>Step 1: Identify 3-5 candidates (Month 1-2)</strong></p> <ul><li>People you already know and trust</li> <li>Have worked with on something real (not just talked politics)</li> <li>Expressed similar concerns about systems</li> <li>Capable of sustained commitment</li></ul> <p><strong>Step 2: Invite to “study group” (Month 2-3)</strong></p> <ul><li>Frame as “figuring out what to do” not “join my organization”</li> <li>Meet bi-weekly to discuss readings, share analysis</li> <li>Test chemistry, commitment, thinking quality</li> <li>Some will drop out naturally (that’s good)</li></ul> <p><strong>Step 3: Propose action project (Month 3-4)</strong></p> <ul><li>“Let’s actually do something small”</li> <li>Could be: Organize event, write proposal, run campaign</li> <li>Test ability to work together under pressure</li> <li>Observe who does what they say, who flakes</li></ul> <p><strong>Step 4: Formalize if working (Month 4-6)</strong></p> <ul><li>“This is working, let’s make it official”</li> <li>Weekly meetings, clear roles, shared accounts</li> <li>Strategic planning, goal-setting</li> <li>Now you have Ring 1</li></ul> <p><strong>Meeting structure for Ring 1:</strong></p> <p><strong>Weekly meeting (90 minutes):</strong></p> <ul><li>Check-in (15 min): Personal updates, capacity this week</li> <li>Strategy (30 min): Big picture, what’s happening, where we’re going</li> <li>Tactics (30 min): Specific tasks, who does what by when</li> <li>Close (15 min): Confirm next steps, appreciation, closing ritual</li></ul> <p><strong>Monthly meeting (3 hours):</strong></p> <ul><li>Review previous month: What worked, what didn’t, lessons</li> <li>Plan next month: Major actions, resource needs, coordination</li> <li>Capacity check: Is anyone burning out, need to adjust</li> <li>Vision refresh: Are we still aligned on goals, strategy</li></ul> <p><strong>Quarterly retreat (full day):</strong></p> <ul><li>Deep strategy review: Are we making progress toward goals?</li> <li>Relationship maintenance: Address tensions, build trust</li> <li>Skills development: Training in organizing, tech, security</li> <li>Celebration: Acknowledge wins, sustain morale</li></ul> <h3>Ring 2: Active Participants — How to Build</h3> <p><strong>Who should be in Ring 2:</strong></p> <p><strong>Criteria:</strong></p> <ul><li>Showed up to 3+ events (demonstrated interest)</li> <li>Contributed something (even small—shared post, brought friend)</li> <li>Aligned with general direction (don’t need full agreement)</li> <li>Reliable enough to assign tasks</li></ul> <p><strong>How to build Ring 2 from Ring 3:</strong></p> <p><strong>The pathway:</strong> Regular participant → Given responsibility → Active participant</p> <p><strong>Month 1-3: Notice who shows up</strong></p> <ul><li>Some people from Ring 3 come to multiple events</li> <li>They’re interested but haven’t been given role yet</li> <li>These are your Ring 2 candidates</li></ul> <p><strong>Month 3-6: Give them specific tasks</strong></p> <ul><li>“Can you help with [concrete thing]?”</li> <li>Start small (bring snacks, share event)</li> <li>Increase responsibility gradually (organize meeting, manage project)</li> <li>Observe: Do they follow through?</li></ul> <p><strong>Month 6-9: Invite to regular team</strong></p> <ul><li>“You’ve been great, want to join planning team?”</li> <li>Not Ring 1 (don’t make strategic decisions)</li> <li>But Ring 2 (execute reliably, trusted with tasks)</li> <li>Meet monthly, coordinate specific projects</li></ul> <p><strong>How to structure Ring 2:</strong></p> <p><strong>Working groups by function:</strong></p> <ul><li>Communications team (social media, newsletters, press)</li> <li>Events team (logistics, turnout, facilitation)</li> <li>Research team (policy analysis, case studies)</li> <li>Fundraising team (grants, donations, events)</li> <li>Tech team (website, tools, security)</li></ul> <p><strong>Each working group:</strong></p> <ul><li>3-7 people</li> <li>One coordinator (reports to Ring 1)</li> <li>Monthly meeting</li> <li>Clear deliverables</li></ul> <p><strong>Communication:</strong></p> <ul><li>Ring 1 ↔ Ring 2 coordinators: Weekly updates</li> <li>Working groups: Internal channels (Slack, Signal)</li> <li>Ring 2 doesn’t know Ring 1 strategic details (unless also in Ring 1)</li></ul> <h3>Ring 3: Supportive Community — How to Build</h3> <p><strong>Who is Ring 3:</strong></p> <ul><li>Anyone interested enough to join mailing list/follow socials</li> <li>Come to occasional events</li> <li>Share content, donate small amounts</li> <li>Provide legitimacy (“we have 500 supporters”)</li></ul> <p><strong>How to build Ring 3:</strong></p> <p><strong>Method 1: Events</strong></p> <ul><li>Host accessible events (talks, workshops, socials)</li> <li>Collect emails/contacts at events</li> <li>Follow up with newsletter invitation</li> <li>Now they’re in Ring 3</li></ul> <p><strong>Method 2: Content</strong></p> <ul><li>Write good analysis (blog, social media)</li> <li>People share, some reach out</li> <li>Invite to newsletter/community</li> <li>Now they’re in Ring 3</li></ul> <p><strong>Method 3: Partnerships</strong></p> <ul><li>Collaborate with aligned organizations</li> <li>Share audiences</li> <li>Some of their people become your Ring 3</li> <li>Now you have access to broader networks</li></ul> <p><strong>How to maintain Ring 3:</strong></p> <p><strong>Email newsletter (monthly):</strong></p> <ul><li>Updates on your work</li> <li>Analysis/educational content</li> <li>Upcoming events/actions</li> <li>Clear calls to action (attend X, share Y, donate Z)</li></ul> <p><strong>Social media (2-3x/week):</strong></p> <ul><li>Mix of educational, mobilizing, celebratory content</li> <li>Not just preaching—respond to people, build relationships</li> <li>Highlight community members, make them feel part of something</li></ul> <p><strong>Big events (quarterly):</strong></p> <ul><li>Open to everyone</li> <li>Shows momentum, builds morale</li> <li>Opportunity to move Ring 3 → Ring 2 (meet active people, see what’s possible)</li></ul> <hr> <h2>The Digital-to-Civic Pipeline</h2> <p><strong>The strategy:</strong> Start digital, move toward physical organizing.</p> <p><strong>Why:</strong> Digital is easy entry (low commitment, low risk). Physical is where real power gets built (high trust, sustained coordination).</p> <p><strong>The pipeline:</strong></p> <p><strong>Stage 1: Digital Connection (Week 1)</strong></p> <ul><li>Someone finds you online (social media, newsletter, Reddit)</li> <li>They lurk, observe, decide if interested</li> <li>Low barrier: Follow, subscribe, join Discord</li></ul> <p><strong>Stage 2: Digital Participation (Week 2-4)</strong></p> <ul><li>They engage with content (comment, share, react)</li> <li>They join discussions (Discord, Twitter threads)</li> <li>Still anonymous, low commitment</li></ul> <p><strong>Stage 3: Digital Contribution (Month 2-3)</strong></p> <ul><li>They create something (write post, make meme, organize thread)</li> <li>They help moderate (Discord, subreddit)</li> <li>They attend online events (Zoom call, livestream)</li> <li>Now they’re invested (created value, built reputation)</li></ul> <p><strong>Stage 4: Local Connection (Month 3-6)</strong></p> <ul><li>You organize local meetup</li> <li>Or they ask “anyone in [city]?”</li> <li>First IRL meeting (coffee, pub, park)</li> <li>Shift from digital → physical</li></ul> <p><strong>Stage 5: Local Action (Month 6-12)</strong></p> <ul><li>They attend local organizing meeting</li> <li>They participate in action (attend council meeting, join protest)</li> <li>They join working group</li> <li>Now they’re Ring 2</li></ul> <p><strong>Stage 6: Core Organizing (Year 2)</strong></p> <ul><li>They prove reliability over time</li> <li>They develop specific skills</li> <li>They’re invited to Ring 1</li> <li>Now they’re co-organizers</li></ul> <p><strong>The key:</strong> Each stage is voluntary. Most people stop at Stage 2-3. That’s fine—they’re Ring 3. The few who go further become your Ring 2 and Ring 1.</p> <p><strong>The infrastructure needed:</strong></p> <p><strong>Digital:</strong></p> <ul><li>Newsletter platform (Substack, Ghost, Buttondown)</li> <li>Social media (Twitter/X, Instagram, TikTok—choose 1-2)</li> <li>Community platform (Discord, Slack, forum)</li> <li>Website (basic landing page, blog)</li></ul> <p><strong>Physical:</strong></p> <ul><li>Regular meetup location (pub, library, park)</li> <li>Contact list (phone numbers for organizing)</li> <li>Working group structure (who does what)</li> <li>Protest/action infrastructure (signs, megaphones, legal support)</li></ul> <p><strong>The transition moments:</strong></p> <p><strong>Digital → Physical invitation:</strong> “Hey [name], I see you’re in [city]. We’re meeting up next week for [casual thing]. Want to come?”</p> <p><strong>Participant → Organizer invitation:</strong> “You’ve been showing up consistently. Want to help plan the next event?”</p> <p><strong>Working group → Core invitation:</strong> “You’ve been great on [team]. Want to join strategy discussions?”</p> <hr> <h2>The Tools: What You Actually Need</h2> <p><strong>Stop overcomplicating this.</strong> You don’t need fancy software or complex infrastructure.</p> <h3>Communication Tools</h3> <p><strong>For Ring 1 (Core Squad):</strong></p> <ul><li><strong>Signal group</strong> (encrypted, self-destructing messages)</li> <li><strong>Shared doc</strong> (Google Doc or Notion for strategy, notes)</li> <li><strong>Video calls</strong> (Zoom, Jitsi for remote members)</li> <li><strong>In-person meetings</strong> (nothing beats face-to-face for trust)</li></ul> <p><strong>For Ring 2 (Active Participants):</strong></p> <ul><li><strong>Discord or Slack</strong> (organized channels, working groups)</li> <li><strong>Task management</strong> (Trello, Asana, or just shared Google Sheet)</li> <li><strong>File sharing</strong> (Google Drive, Dropbox—centralized resources)</li></ul> <p><strong>For Ring 3 (Supportive Community):</strong></p> <ul><li><strong>Email newsletter</strong> (Substack free tier, Mailchimp)</li> <li><strong>Social media</strong> (Twitter/X, Instagram—wherever your people are)</li> <li><strong>Simple website</strong> (Carrd, Webflow, or GitHub Pages—just needs landing page)</li></ul> <h3>Organizing Tools</h3> <p><strong>Event organizing:</strong></p> <ul><li><strong>Eventbrite or Luma</strong> (free tiers exist, easy RSVPs)</li> <li><strong>Google Forms</strong> (for surveys, sign-ups)</li> <li><strong>WhatsApp/Signal groups</strong> (event-specific coordination)</li></ul> <p><strong>Resource management:</strong></p> <ul><li><strong>Shared calendar</strong> (Google Calendar, Calendly for scheduling)</li> <li><strong>Spreadsheets</strong> (track contacts, tasks, budgets)</li> <li><strong>Cloud storage</strong> (templates, graphics, documents)</li></ul> <p><strong>Security tools:</strong></p> <ul><li><strong>Signal</strong> (encrypted messaging—use for anything sensitive)</li> <li><strong>ProtonMail</strong> (encrypted email if needed)</li> <li><strong>VPN</strong> (basic protection)</li> <li><strong>Password manager</strong> (1Password, Bitwarden)</li></ul> <p><strong>The principle:</strong> Start minimal. Add tools only when current ones break. Complexity is your enemy.</p> <hr> <h2>The First 90 Days: Your Squad Launch Sequence</h2> <p>You’ve read the theory. Here’s what you do starting Monday:</p> <h3>Week 1: Audit Your Networks</h3> <p><strong>Day 1-2: List your networks</strong></p> <ul class="contains-task-list"><li class="task-list-item"><input type="checkbox" disabled> Write down every group you’re part of (gaming, gym, parents, professional, neighborhood)</li> <li class="task-list-item"><input type="checkbox" disabled> For each: How many people? How often meet? What’s the activity?</li> <li class="task-list-item"><input type="checkbox" disabled> Rate trust level (1-10) and political potential (1-10)</li></ul> <p><strong>Day 3-4: Identify 3-5 core people</strong></p> <ul class="contains-task-list"><li class="task-list-item"><input type="checkbox" disabled> From your networks, who fits Ring 1 criteria?</li> <li class="task-list-item"><input type="checkbox" disabled> Who have you worked with successfully before?</li> <li class="task-list-item"><input type="checkbox" disabled> Who shares your concerns about systems?</li> <li class="task-list-item"><input type="checkbox" disabled> Who could you call right now and invite to coffee?</li></ul> <p><strong>Day 5: Make contact</strong></p> <ul class="contains-task-list"><li class="task-list-item"><input type="checkbox" disabled> Message 3-5 people: “Want to grab coffee this week? Been thinking about [shared concern], want to talk through some ideas.”</li> <li class="task-list-item"><input type="checkbox" disabled> No big pitch yet—just gauge interest, build rapport</li></ul> <p><strong>Output:</strong> By end of Week 1, you have coffee scheduled with 3-5 potential Ring 1 people.</p> <h3>Week 2-3: Form Study Group</h3> <p><strong>Week 2: First meeting</strong></p> <ul class="contains-task-list"><li class="task-list-item"><input type="checkbox" disabled> Meet in casual setting (coffee shop, pub, someone’s home)</li> <li class="task-list-item"><input type="checkbox" disabled> Share concerns: “What’s been bothering you about [systems/society/world]?”</li> <li class="task-list-item"><input type="checkbox" disabled> Listen more than talk—discover shared analysis</li> <li class="task-list-item"><input type="checkbox" disabled> End with: “Want to keep meeting? Maybe bi-weekly to figure this out?”</li></ul> <p><strong>Week 3: Second meeting</strong></p> <ul class="contains-task-list"><li class="task-list-item"><input type="checkbox" disabled> Pick something to read/watch together (article, book chapter, documentary)</li> <li class="task-list-item"><input type="checkbox" disabled> Discuss analysis: What rings true? What’s missing?</li> <li class="task-list-item"><input type="checkbox" disabled> Start building shared language, framework</li> <li class="task-list-item"><input type="checkbox" disabled> Propose: “Should we do something, not just talk?”</li></ul> <p><strong>Output:</strong> By end of Week 3, you have 3-5 people meeting regularly, developing shared analysis.</p> <h3>Week 4-6: First Action Project</h3> <p><strong>Week 4: Choose project</strong></p> <ul class="contains-task-list"><li class="task-list-item"><input type="checkbox" disabled> Something small, achievable, relevant to shared concerns</li> <li class="task-list-item"><input type="checkbox" disabled> Examples: Organize event, write analysis, petition council, start newsletter</li> <li class="task-list-item"><input type="checkbox" disabled> Assign roles based on skills</li> <li class="task-list-item"><input type="checkbox" disabled> Set deadline (4 weeks out)</li></ul> <p><strong>Week 5: Work on project</strong></p> <ul class="contains-task-list"><li class="task-list-item"><input type="checkbox" disabled> Check in mid-week (text/call)</li> <li class="task-list-item"><input type="checkbox" disabled> Meet if needed (work session)</li> <li class="task-list-item"><input type="checkbox" disabled> Adjust if hitting obstacles</li></ul> <p><strong>Week 6: Complete & review</strong></p> <ul class="contains-task-list"><li class="task-list-item"><input type="checkbox" disabled> Finish project (event held, article published, etc.)</li> <li class="task-list-item"><input type="checkbox" disabled> Meet to review: What worked? What didn’t? Want to keep going?</li> <li class="task-list-item"><input type="checkbox" disabled> If yes: “Let’s formalize—weekly meetings, actual organizing”</li></ul> <p><strong>Output:</strong> By end of Week 6, you’ve completed first project together, proven you can work as team.</p> <h3>Week 7-9: Structure Ring 1</h3> <p><strong>Week 7: Establish rhythm</strong></p> <ul class="contains-task-list"><li class="task-list-item"><input type="checkbox" disabled> Set weekly meeting time (same day/time each week)</li> <li class="task-list-item"><input type="checkbox" disabled> Create Signal group for core squad</li> <li class="task-list-item"><input type="checkbox" disabled> Set up shared doc for notes, strategy</li></ul> <p><strong>Week 8: Define roles</strong></p> <ul class="contains-task-list"><li class="task-list-item"><input type="checkbox" disabled> Who’s good at what? (organizing, writing, tech, fundraising, strategy)</li> <li class="task-list-item"><input type="checkbox" disabled> Rotate facilitator role (don’t let one person dominate)</li> <li class="task-list-item"><input type="checkbox" disabled> Agree on decision-making process (consensus, voting, delegated)</li></ul> <p><strong>Week 9: Set goals</strong></p> <ul class="contains-task-list"><li class="task-list-item"><input type="checkbox" disabled> 3-month goals: What do we want to achieve?</li> <li class="task-list-item"><input type="checkbox" disabled> 6-month vision: Where could this go?</li> <li class="task-list-item"><input type="checkbox" disabled> 12-month dream: What’s the ambitious version?</li></ul> <p><strong>Output:</strong> By end of Week 9, you have functioning Ring 1 with regular meetings, clear roles, shared goals.</p> <h3>Week 10-12: Start Building Ring 2</h3> <p><strong>Week 10: Plan public event</strong></p> <ul class="contains-task-list"><li class="task-list-item"><input type="checkbox" disabled> Something accessible (talk, workshop, social)</li> <li class="task-list-item"><input type="checkbox" disabled> Invite your networks (gaming Discord, gym crew, parents group)</li> <li class="task-list-item"><input type="checkbox" disabled> Create Facebook event, share widely</li> <li class="task-list-item"><input type="checkbox" disabled> Low-key—“Come hang out, meet people thinking about [topic]”</li></ul> <p><strong>Week 11: Promote & prep</strong></p> <ul class="contains-task-list"><li class="task-list-item"><input type="checkbox" disabled> Share event in all your networks</li> <li class="task-list-item"><input type="checkbox" disabled> Prepare materials (if talk: slides; if workshop: handouts)</li> <li class="task-list-item"><input type="checkbox" disabled> Assign roles (greeter, facilitator, note-taker)</li></ul> <p><strong>Week 12: Hold event & follow up</strong></p> <ul class="contains-task-list"><li class="task-list-item"><input type="checkbox" disabled> Run event (15-30 people is success for first one)</li> <li class="task-list-item"><input type="checkbox" disabled> Collect emails/contacts (signup sheet)</li> <li class="task-list-item"><input type="checkbox" disabled> Send follow-up within 48 hours</li> <li class="task-list-item"><input type="checkbox" disabled> Invite to next event (monthly rhythm)</li></ul> <p><strong>Output:</strong> By end of Week 12, you have 20-40 people aware of you (Ring 3 beginning), 5-10 who came to event (potential Ring 2).</p> <h3>Beyond 90 Days: The Growth Loop</h3> <p><strong>Month 4-6: Establish Ring 2</strong></p> <ul><li>Monthly events (build Ring 3)</li> <li>Invite consistent attendees to help organize (build Ring 2)</li> <li>Form working groups (communications, events, etc.)</li></ul> <p><strong>Month 6-12: Scale coordination</strong></p> <ul><li>Weekly Ring 1 meetings (strategy)</li> <li>Monthly Ring 2 meetings (coordination)</li> <li>Quarterly Ring 3 events (mobilization)</li> <li>Consistent output (newsletter, social media, actions)</li></ul> <p><strong>Year 2: Consolidate power</strong></p> <ul><li>Ring 1: 5-15 people (core organizers)</li> <li>Ring 2: 30-60 people (active participants)</li> <li>Ring 3: 200-500 people (supportive community)</li> <li>Infrastructure: Regular meetings, working groups, fundraising, political wins</li></ul> <p><strong>The goal:</strong> By Year 2, you have organized political force capable of:</p> <ul><li>Coordinating 50+ people for actions</li> <li>Influencing local politics (council decisions, elections)</li> <li>Building alternative institutions (cooperatives, land trusts)</li> <li>Supporting members economically (mutual aid, jobs)</li></ul> <hr> <h2>Common Pitfalls and How to Avoid Them</h2> <h3>Pitfall 1: Moving Too Fast</h3> <p><strong>The mistake:</strong> Week 1 you announce “We’re building revolutionary organization!”</p> <p><strong>Why it fails:</strong></p> <ul><li>Scares off potential allies</li> <li>Attracts ego-driven people (want to be revolutionary heroes)</li> <li>Skips trust-building stage</li> <li>Creates security risks</li></ul> <p><strong>The fix:</strong></p> <ul><li>Start with activity stage (just do the thing together)</li> <li>Let identity emerge organically (we are X people)</li> <li>Only introduce politics once trust is high</li></ul> <p><strong>The test:</strong> If you’re announcing your politics before people trust you personally, you’re moving too fast.</p> <h3>Pitfall 2: No Structure</h3> <p><strong>The mistake:</strong> “We’re horizontal, no hierarchy, everyone’s equal”</p> <p><strong>Why it fails:</strong></p> <ul><li>Decisions take forever (endless meetings)</li> <li>Charismatic people dominate anyway (informal hierarchy)</li> <li>Nothing gets done (no one accountable)</li> <li>Burnout (organizers doing everything)</li></ul> <p><strong>The fix:</strong></p> <ul><li>Clear roles (who does what)</li> <li>Decision-making process (how we decide)</li> <li>Ring structure (different levels of commitment/responsibility)</li> <li>Rotating facilitation (spread burden)</li></ul> <p><strong>The test:</strong> If meetings go in circles and nothing gets decided, you need more structure.</p> <h3>Pitfall 3: Too Much Structure</h3> <p><strong>The mistake:</strong> Bylaws, committees, parliamentary procedure from Day 1</p> <p><strong>Why it fails:</strong></p> <ul><li>Bureaucracy kills energy</li> <li>Formality prevents personal relationships</li> <li>Structure becomes end in itself</li> <li>Feels like job, not movement</li></ul> <p><strong>The fix:</strong></p> <ul><li>Start informal (just friends working together)</li> <li>Add structure only when current approach breaks</li> <li>Keep it minimal (only structure that serves action)</li> <li>Maintain social bonds (not just meetings—hang out)</li></ul> <p><strong>The test:</strong> If people avoid meetings because they’re tedious, you have too much structure.</p> <h3>Pitfall 4: Ideological Purity</h3> <p><strong>The mistake:</strong> “Only join if you agree with our 47-point manifesto”</p> <p><strong>Why it fails:</strong></p> <ul><li>Tiny membership (most people don’t have fully formed ideology)</li> <li>Echo chamber (everyone thinks exactly alike)</li> <li>Splits over minor disagreements (purity spirals)</li> <li>No room for learning/growth</li></ul> <p><strong>The fix:</strong></p> <ul><li>Start with shared concerns, not ideology</li> <li>Welcome people at different developmental stages</li> <li>Focus on actions, not beliefs</li> <li>Allow disagreement (productive conflict is healthy)</li></ul> <p><strong>The test:</strong> If everyone agrees on everything, you’re probably in purity trap.</p> <h3>Pitfall 5: Savior Complex</h3> <p><strong>The mistake:</strong> One person trying to organize everyone else</p> <p><strong>Why it fails:</strong></p> <ul><li>Burnout (one person doing everything)</li> <li>Resentment (others feel like clients, not co-creators)</li> <li>Fragility (if organizer quits, everything collapses)</li> <li>Power dynamics (unhealthy dependency)</li></ul> <p><strong>The fix:</strong></p> <ul><li>Share organizing labor from start</li> <li>Rotate facilitation, leadership roles</li> <li>Develop others’ skills (teach, don’t do for them)</li> <li>Take breaks (model sustainability)</li></ul> <p><strong>The test:</strong> If you’re exhausted and others are passive, you have savior complex.</p> <h3>Pitfall 6: Martyrdom Culture</h3> <p><strong>The mistake:</strong> “Real activists sacrifice everything for the cause”</p> <p><strong>Why it fails:</strong></p> <ul><li>Burnout epidemic</li> <li>Unsustainable (people can’t maintain forever)</li> <li>Reproduces exploitation (movement version)</li> <li>Excludes people with care responsibilities</li></ul> <p><strong>The fix:</strong></p> <ul><li>Celebrate rest, boundaries, saying no</li> <li>Share burden (rotate heavy tasks)</li> <li>Check capacity (ask “what can you do this week?“)</li> <li>Model sustainability (organizers take breaks)</li></ul> <p><strong>The test:</strong> If everyone’s exhausted and proud of it, you have martyrdom culture.</p> <h3>Pitfall 7: Talking Without Action</h3> <p><strong>The mistake:</strong> Endless meetings, perfect plans, never actually doing anything</p> <p><strong>Why it fails:</strong></p> <ul><li>Morale collapses (no wins)</li> <li>People lose interest (just talk is boring)</li> <li>No learning (you learn by doing, not planning)</li> <li>Vulnerability to infiltration (talkers are easy to neutralize)</li></ul> <p><strong>The fix:</strong></p> <ul><li>Action bias (do something small this week)</li> <li>Imperfect action > perfect planning</li> <li>Test and iterate (start small, learn, adjust)</li> <li>Celebrate wins (even tiny ones maintain morale)</li></ul> <p><strong>The test:</strong> If you’ve been meeting 3 months with no external action, you’re in talking trap.</p> <hr> <h2>Security Culture: Protecting Your Squad</h2> <p><strong>The reality:</strong> If you’re effective, you’ll face opposition. Could be:</p> <ul><li>Employer firing organizers</li> <li>Landlords evicting activists</li> <li>Police monitoring/disrupting</li> <li>Infiltrators gathering intelligence</li> <li>Media hit pieces delegitimizing</li></ul> <p><strong>The solution:</strong> Security culture. Not paranoia—competent protection.</p> <h3>Basic Security Practices</h3> <p><strong>For Ring 1 (Core Squad):</strong></p> <p><strong>Communication security:</strong></p> <ul><li>Use Signal for sensitive discussions</li> <li>Enable disappearing messages (24-hour timer)</li> <li>Don’t discuss strategy on Facebook, email, SMS</li> <li>In-person meetings for most sensitive topics</li></ul> <p><strong>Operational security:</strong></p> <ul><li>Don’t announce plans before they happen</li> <li>Need-to-know basis (Ring 2 doesn’t know Ring 1 strategy)</li> <li>Compartmentalize (working groups don’t know each other’s tasks)</li> <li>Security check before expanding Ring 1 (trust, but verify)</li></ul> <p><strong>Digital security:</strong></p> <ul><li>Use strong, unique passwords (password manager)</li> <li>Enable 2FA on all accounts</li> <li>Don’t use work email/devices for organizing</li> <li>Assume surveillance (act accordingly)</li></ul> <p><strong>Physical security:</strong></p> <ul><li>Vary meeting locations (don’t always meet same place)</li> <li>Notice who’s around (surveillance, infiltrators)</li> <li>Have exit plans (what if meeting raided)</li> <li>Don’t bring unnecessary devices (phones can be tracked)</li></ul> <p><strong>For Ring 2 (Active Participants):</strong></p> <p><strong>Communications:</strong></p> <ul><li>Discord/Slack for general coordination (not sensitive stuff)</li> <li>Signal for action-specific groups</li> <li>Remind people: Don’t share sensitive info publicly</li></ul> <p><strong>Actions:</strong></p> <ul><li>Legal support contacts (before action, not during)</li> <li>Know your rights (what to say to police: nothing)</li> <li>Buddy system (don’t go to actions alone)</li> <li>Debrief after actions (what went well, what didn’t)</li></ul> <p><strong>For Ring 3 (Supportive Community):</strong></p> <p><strong>Public messaging:</strong></p> <ul><li>Frame carefully (avoid giving ammunition to opponents)</li> <li>Don’t over-share (they don’t need to know internal dynamics)</li> <li>Vet before sharing sensitive content</li> <li>Assume anything public will be seen by opponents</li></ul> <h3>Dealing With Infiltrators</h3> <p><strong>The reality:</strong> If you’re effective, infiltrators will show up. Could be:</p> <ul><li>Police/security services (monitoring, disrupting)</li> <li>Employers (gathering intel on worker organizing)</li> <li>Political opponents (trying to cause drama)</li> <li>Bad actors (mentally ill, provocateurs)</li></ul> <p><strong>How to spot infiltrators:</strong></p> <p><strong>Red flags:</strong></p> <ul><li>Pushing for illegal action (agent provocateur)</li> <li>Asking for membership lists, contact info</li> <li>Wanting to know everything about strategy</li> <li>Creating interpersonal drama, conflict</li> <li>Suddenly very committed with no history</li> <li>Asking about other people’s activities</li></ul> <p><strong>What to do:</strong></p> <ul><li>Don’t accuse (creates paranoia)</li> <li>Limit their access (keep in Ring 3, not Ring 2/1)</li> <li>Feed them harmless info (let them report nothing useful)</li> <li>Document behavior (in case need to remove)</li> <li>If confirmed: Remove calmly, no drama</li></ul> <p><strong>Prevention:</strong></p> <ul><li>Slow onboarding (prove trust over time)</li> <li>Ring structure (sensitive info only at Ring 1)</li> <li>Competence test (do they actually do useful work?)</li> <li>Background check (do they have history of organizing?)</li></ul> <h3>Legal Support</h3> <p><strong>Before you need it:</strong></p> <ul class="contains-task-list"><li class="task-list-item"><input type="checkbox" disabled> Identify legal support (activist lawyers, legal observers)</li> <li class="task-list-item"><input type="checkbox" disabled> Know your rights (what to say during arrest: nothing)</li> <li class="task-list-item"><input type="checkbox" disabled> Have bail fund (money set aside for arrests)</li> <li class="task-list-item"><input type="checkbox" disabled> Emergency contacts (who to call if arrested)</li></ul> <p><strong>During confrontation:</strong></p> <ul><li>Don’t talk to police without lawyer</li> <li>Record interactions (but securely)</li> <li>Stay calm (don’t escalate)</li> <li>Stick together (don’t leave people behind)</li></ul> <p><strong>After incident:</strong></p> <ul><li>Debrief quickly (while memory fresh)</li> <li>Document everything (injuries, what happened)</li> <li>Contact legal support immediately</li> <li>Don’t discuss on social media (wait for lawyer)</li></ul> <hr> <h2>What Chapter 17 Delivers</h2> <p>You now understand people-level organizing:</p> <ul><li>How to activate existing networks (5 types)</li> <li>How to structure coordination (3 rings)</li> <li>How to scale from 20 to 2,000 (digital-to-civic pipeline)</li> <li>How to avoid common pitfalls</li> <li>How to maintain security</li></ul> <p><strong>Chapter 17 is about specific targets:</strong></p> <ul><li>Ten named UK cities with exact playbooks</li> <li>Which councillors to contact</li> <li>Which sanctuaries to build first</li> <li>Which crises to exploit</li> <li>Concrete timelines, budgets, contact info</li></ul> <p><strong>The connection:</strong></p> <ul><li>Chapter 15 (Trojan Horses) gets you inside institutions</li> <li>Chapter 16 (Building the Squad) gives you organized people</li> <li>Chapter 17 (Pilot Cities) tells you exactly where to deploy both</li> <li>Together: You have institutions, people, and targets—everything needed for power</li></ul> <hr> <p><strong>REALITY CHECK: THE SQUAD READINESS TEST</strong></p> <p><strong>Question 1:</strong> Do you have 3-5 people you trust enough to form Ring 1?</p> <p>If no, go back to Week 1 of this chapter. Identify candidates. Start building trust.</p> <p><strong>Question 2:</strong> Can you organize a simple event and get 15+ people to show up?</p> <p>If no, practice. Host game night, workout session, coffee meetup. Build coordination muscles.</p> <p><strong>Question 3:</strong> Can you maintain operational security while building public presence?</p> <p>If no, study security section again. Start with Signal, strong passwords, need-to-know basis.</p> <p><strong>If yes to all three:</strong> You’re ready to build organized political force.</p> <p><strong>If no to any:</strong> Work on that capacity before scaling.</p> <hr> <p><strong>You now understand how to turn networks into organized power.</strong></p> <p>The question is: Will you do the patient work of trust-building, skill-developing, and structure-creating?</p> <p>Because the oligarchy doesn’t care if you understand organizing theory.</p> <p>They care when you coordinate 50 people to pack a council meeting, when your gaming Discord becomes mutual aid network, when your gym crew forms worker cooperative, when your parents group takes over the school board.</p> <p><strong>That’s when they realize you’re actually dangerous.</strong></p> <hr> <p><strong>Next: Chapter 17 - Pilot Cities 2026-2030</strong></p> <p><em>Ten named UK cities with exact playbooks—which councillors, which sanctuaries, which crises, which timeline</em></p> <p>(Bradford, Bristol, Preston, Dundee, Liverpool, Sheffield, Newcastle, Cardiff, Belfast, Glasgow—and why these ten are perfect launch targets for systemic transformation.)</p>`,1);function g(e){var i=n();l(1072),o(e,i)}export{g as default};
